  "final_text": "Wrote `impact.txt` with 12 entries.\n\nHow I got them: `who` on `src/attr/_make.py#_OBJ_SETATTR` reported 15 backlinks with `coverage.complete = true` over the whole repo (140 files, one revision) \u2014 so every reference in the repository was attributed. I dropped three of the 15:\n\n- `src/attr/_funcs.py` (module page) \u2014 that's the import line only.\n- `src/attr/_make.py#_ClassBuilder` and `#Attribute` (class pages) \u2014 I read both class sources to confirm neither class body uses the name; those hits are aggregates of their methods, which are already listed individually.\n\nThe result:\n\n| file | definitions |\n|---|---|\n| `src/attr/_funcs.py` | `assoc`, `resolve_types` |\n| `src/attr/_make.py` | `_transform_attrs`, `_make_cached_property_getattr`, `_make_init_script`, `_ClassBuilder._patch_original_class`, `_ClassBuilder._create_slots_class`, `_ClassBuilder._make_getstate_setstate`, `_ClassBuilder.add_setattr`, `Attribute.__init__`, `Attribute.evolve`, `Attribute._setattrs` |\n\nNo test file uses the name \u2014 nothing in `tests/` references it (the index resolves module-attribute access such as `_make._OBJ_SETATTR`, so a test use would have shown up).\n\nTwo things worth flagging:\n\n- Where a use sits inside a nested function, I named the enclosing addressable definition, not the nested one: `_ClassBuilder._make_getstate_setstate` (use is in the nested `slots_setstate`) and `_ClassBuilder.add_setattr` (use is in the nested `__setattr__`).\n- Some uses are indirect for a rename: `_make_cached_property_getattr` and `_make_init_script` pass `_OBJ_SETATTR.__get__` into a generated-code globals dict under the name `_cached_setattr_get`, and the generated source strings refer to *that* name. Renaming the constant doesn't require touching those strings.",
